{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Jethro rejoiced.</b> From the root of חדוה (joy). It ought to have been on the pattern of וַיִפְתְּ בסתר לבי (Job 31:27) and וַיִשְׁבְּ ממנו שבי (Numbers 21:1). Only the chet was opened with a patach because it is a guttural letter, and the dalet remained dagesh as it had been. And I do not know in all of Scripture a dagesh letter at the end of a word, except if it is a bet/vet/gimel/dalet/kaf/pe/tav where two shvas combine at the end, such as וַיִשְׁבְּ ממנו (there) and וַיֵשְׁתְּ מן היין (Genesis 9:21). But with one of the letters [that take a dagesh] we do not find it dagesh but only with a patach, and they are two: וַיִחַדְ יתרו and אל יִחַד בימי שנה (Job 3:6)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Resolved 'the letters [that take a dagesh]' as referring back to the בג\"ד כפ\"ת group mentioned earlier; the phrase 'עם אחד מהמלכים' literally reads 'with one of the kings' but contextually means 'with one of the [seven] letters' — reading מלכים as a term for letters that receive dagesh."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:33.884072948Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}